---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import BusinessPerspectiveNote from '../../components/BusinessPerspectiveNote.astro';
import ToolNextStep from '../../components/ToolNextStep.astro';
import EducationPaybackPlanner from '../../components/EducationPaybackPlanner.astro';
const title = 'Education Cost and Payback Planner for Indian Families';
const description = 'See what a course really costs, how long it may take to earn that money back, and what to check before you pay. Free and private.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/tools/education-cost-payback-planner/';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Nav /><BusinessPerspectiveNote /><main class="tool-page"><div class="tool-shell">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span><a href="/career-resources/">Career resources</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span><span aria-current="page">Education payback planner</span></nav>
    <p class="eyebrow">PRIVATE BROWSER-BASED PLANNING TOOL</p><h1>What will this study route really cost?</h1>
    <p class="lead">Enter the numbers your family is considering. You get what the course costs, what you give up while studying, how long it may take to earn the money back, and three next steps you can copy to WhatsApp.</p>
    <EducationPaybackPlanner />
    <section class="tool-notes"><h2>Use it as a stress test</h2><p>Run the route three times: with the pay you expect, with a lower starting pay and with a later start. The result table keeps each run, so you can compare it with a lower-cost course or a work-first route.</p><p>The planner leaves out loan interest. Work that out with the <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/tools/education-loan-emi-calculator/">education loan EMI calculator</a>. If you only know an offer's CTC, turn it into monthly in-hand pay with the <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/tools/ctc-to-in-hand-salary-calculator/">CTC to in-hand salary calculator</a>.</p>
    <h2>A worked example (an illustration, not real fees)</h2><p>A family looks at a 4-year degree with ₹4,00,000 of fees and ₹6,000 a month for living and travel. That is ₹4,00,000 plus ₹2,88,000, so ₹6,88,000 is spent on the course.</p><p>If the first job gives ₹30,000 in hand and essentials take ₹15,000, ₹15,000 is left each month. The ₹6,88,000 then comes back in about 46 months, or 3.8 years, if every spare rupee goes to it. With only half the spare money it takes 7.6 years, which shows why the first-year pay figure matters most.</p>
    <h2>When to talk to a person</h2><p>Before you borrow or pay a large deposit, ask the college accounts office for the fee schedule in writing. A bank officer can explain the loan offer, and a counsellor can help compare routes.</p>
    <p><a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/parents/how-much-to-spend-on-child-education-india/">Read the education-spend guide</a> · <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/career-chart-after-12th/">Compare routes after 12th</a></p></section>
  </div>  <ToolNextStep toolName="Education Cost and Payback Planner" audience="parent" />
  </main><Footer />
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .tool-page{min-height:70vh;padding:4rem 1.2rem;background:radial-gradient(circle at 20% 0%,rgba(18,154,141,.12),transparent 35%),#080c18}.tool-shell{max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto}.breadcrumb{display:flex;gap:.55rem;flex-wrap:wrap;color:rgba(255,255,255,.55);font-size:.85rem;margin-bottom:2.5rem}.breadcrumb a,.tool-notes a{color:#8de4d8;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px}.eyebrow{color:#e5b84a;font-size:.8rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.12em}.tool-page h1{font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,4rem);margin:.65rem 0 1rem;color:#fff}.lead{max-width:800px;font-size:1.15rem;line-height:1.7;color:rgba(255,255,255,.76)}.tool-notes{margin-top:2rem;padding:1.2rem;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.15);border-radius:16px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.75);line-height:1.7}.tool-notes h2{color:#fff}
</style>
